WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.060
Stephen King's It is a wildly ambitious story,
and there are plenty of details you may not

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.620
be aware of, or things you've simply forgotten
about the first installment.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.280
Here's a look at everything you need to know
before seeing It: Chapter Two.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:13.800
"You'll float too.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.220
You'll float too.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:16.660
You'll float too.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.280
You'll float too…"

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.100
At the end of It, the kids cut their hands
and swear a blood oath to one another, promising

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:25.710
to return to Derry if the creature ever returns.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:30.200
It Chapter Two sees that blood oath realized,
as the creature commonly known as Pennywise

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.900
the Dancing Clown does indeed resurface after
27 years.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:36.960
"Hello."

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.900
When It returns, the Losers Club is scattered
across the United States, with its members

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.469
all leading very different lives.

00:00:43.469 --> 00:00:46.960
In the book, Bill Denbrough has become a bestselling
suspense novelist.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.200
Ben Hanscom is a successful architect living
in Nebraska.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.440
Richie Tozier is a DJ in Los Angeles.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.260
Eddie Kaspbrak runs a limousine company in
New York City.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.500
Stanley Uris is a partner at an accounting
firm in Atlanta.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:03.100
Beverly Marsh is a successful fashion designer
living in Chicago and married to an abusive

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:03.600
husband.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.540
"This meeting of The Losers Club has officially
begun."

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.580
Though most of the Losers Club moved away
from Derry, one member stayed behind: Mike

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.380
Hanlon, the orphan boy whose family's history
is deeply tied to the violence and hatred

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.620
brought on by Pennywise.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.420
Since Mike stayed in Derry, he sacrificed
his own potential, because the creature's

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:31.310
supernatural influence acts as a kind of suppressant.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.740
Mike becomes the historian of the group, and
of It.

00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:38.620
In King's novel, he keeps a detailed journal
filled with research about what It is.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:42.310
When the creature's feeding cycle starts again,
Mike is the person who reaches out to the

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:46.220
other members of the Losers Club, reminding
them of the oath they took and asking them

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:47.100
to come back and help.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.940
Without spoiling anything, we'll just say
it's a life that definitely takes a toll on

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:51.440
Mike.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.439
Since It Chapter Two is a sequel, it obviously
expands the world of the original film, and

00:01:56.439 --> 00:01:58.850
there are plenty of fresh faces in the movie,
too.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.520
Well, maybe "fresh" isn't quite the word we're
looking for here.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.760
"But tell me, how is it being back in Derry?"

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.871
While Bill Skarsgård will definitely be returning
to play Pennywise the Dancing Clown, the film

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.620
also features a frightening new form for the
creature: Mrs. Kersh.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.560
She's a seemingly sweet elderly woman who
lives in Beverly's old apartment and she's

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:25.000
prominently featured in the film's first trailer.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.290
Despite initial appearances, she is not a
friendly presence.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:31.300
"I was always daddy's little girl.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:32.960
What about you?"

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.060
In the novel, three homophobic teenagers attack
a gay man named Adrian Mellon, portrayed in

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:42.909
It Chapter Two by actor, writer, and director
Xavier Dolan.

00:02:42.909 --> 00:02:47.100
In the book, Mellon is thrown off a bridge
and subsequently attacked and killed by Pennywise.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:51.060
This is one of the most disturbing scenes
in King's massive novel, and it wasn't included

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.340
in the 1990 miniseries adaptation.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:57.580
But screenwriter Gary Dauberman has confirmed
the hate crime will definitely be featured

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:58.840
in It Chapter Two.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.180
He told The Hollywood Reporter:

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:04.940
"It is the first attack in present-day Derry
and sets the stage for what Derry has become...It's

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.680
pure evil what happens to Adrian.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.000
These bullies working through Pennywise was
important for us to show."

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:15.780
It was a very dark movie, featuring a child
murder in the first 10 minutes...and a terrifying

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.960
trip into the Derry sewers to fight the monster
in the film's finale.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:30.319
According to screenwriter Gary Dauberman,
It Chapter Two effectively doubles down on

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:30.820
the horror.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.060
He told SyFy Wire:

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.749
"I think it's darker in a lot of ways than
the first one.

00:03:34.749 --> 00:03:36.780
The two movies feel like a complete whole
to me."

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.870
According to Pennywise actor Bill Skarsgård,
a lot of the horror stems from the villain's

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:41.520
new mindset.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:45.840
He wasn't killed by the Losers Club, but he
was wounded and felt fear for the very first

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:46.620
time in his life.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.420
Skarsgård told Entertainment Weekly:

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:52.600
"[Fear] fuels hatred and anger towards the
kids, who will be adults in this one, so I

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:54.700
think there might be an even more vicious
Pennywise.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.180
He’s really going after it."

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:01.740
The first It recreated a number of key moments
from King's novel, including a famous sequence

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.060
in which Beverly Marsh is tormented by Pennywise
in her bathroom.

00:04:05.329 --> 00:04:08.900
This results in gallons of blood shooting
out of the sink that her father can't see.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.920
But the blood is clearly visible to the other
members of the Losers Club, who clean every

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:13.920
inch of the bathroom.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.520
It's a very bloody scene, but apparently It
Chapter Two is going to be even bloodier.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:23.320
Actress Jessica Chastain, who plays the adult
Beverly Marsh, described an extremely gory

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:25.550
sequence during an interview with Jimmy Fallon:

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:30.030
"There's a scene that someone said on set
that it's the most blood that's ever been

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:33.180
in a horror film, in a scene."

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:37.450
We don't know the context of the scene yet,
but the first Chapter Two trailer does feature

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:40.960
Beverly swimming through what seems to be
a literal room of blood.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:47.840
We'll find out for sure how much blood is
being spilled when It Chapter Two lunges into

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:49.600
theaters on September 6th.

